A||||5||btoth||stalling while spinning||||7||
Z||000000||btoth||11-29-2000||03:58 PM||||I "somehow"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> managed to spin my car around today and it stalled.  Does this happen just because the wheels end up spinning backwards while it's in Drive?  (This is the first time I've spun it on partially dry ground as opposed to snow/ice.)  BTW, how bad is this on an auto trans?  TIA.||||reg||7
Z||000001||Raydar||11-29-2000||05:21 PM||||If the wheels end up turning backwards, it can stall the engine. The torque converter will only slip so much. Probably didn't hurt anything. Probably won't hurt anything the second time. Or the third. But it <I>can't</I> be too good for it to keep doing it. I'd be more worried about all the stuff that you could slide into.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>Raydar   ||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||11-29-2000||06:16 PM||||It probably didn't do too much, but I wouldn't do the same thing as much.  If you like doing these donut things, like everybody does, try shifting it into neutral before you get around all the way.  It's easier when you have an auto.  Then you just have to throw it down, and not worry.  You could slide farther or something, so it is a little more dangerous, but it won't hurt the car as much.  Anybody try e-brake turns?  Those are fun.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000003||jlhuber||11-29-2000||06:33 PM||||in '98, i was in the parking lot of a department store (Kmart) at about 12:00pm having some fum.  it was the night of the local friday night car show.  the parking lot ashphalt (sp?) had been re-sealed about two weeks prior.  anyways, after everyone had left, i decided to show my cousin (an anti-fiero type) what my stock '86 gt was.  from a dead stop, hit the gas, rear tires broke loose, turned hard left, spun a 360, slid backwards while holding 3/4 throttle, and tires spinning foreward smoking.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  after we were done we got out to, *ahem* survey, the damage.  well, my cousin is now a believer (sp?).  he never thought a fiero could slide backwards on dry pavement (granted it had been sealed recently) with the tires going the opposite direction.  we both agreed that my little pocket rocket was a decent "bang for the buck".  course, the cop that had been sitting right there watching us the whole time with all his lights off thought it was more of a bright red hot-rod that was going to get me into serious trouble.  he was kind enough to let me off with a warning of "be more careful next time, those wreckless driving tickets are pricey".  hehe, some cops are cool, makes me wonder if maybe he was a believer too ...you never know.  moral of the story, scope the place out before you let the animal out.  later.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Raydar||11-30-2000||01:51 PM||||Knew a guy with a 73 340 Challenger that usta do donuts in parking lots. <BR>Till he hit that lamp post. <BR>DOH!!!||||reg||6
Z||000005||onfire||11-30-2000||02:24 PM||||Please don't try this at home.<P>My first car was a 78 Trans-Am and we used to sit on the trunk the rear spoiler held you in.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. And we used to do doughnuts in the snow with someone laying on the trunk. Well one time my friend hit a curb, with me on the back. Picture me rolling off the back and onto 6 inches of snow. I didn't get hurt it was actually kinda of funny, now. He wasn't going that fast anyway. That was like 12yrs. ago. My how time flies.<P><BR>------------------<BR>"I seen it on the internet, and I believe everthing on the net."<BR><A HREF="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/on_fire.html" TARGET=_blank>Check out my waist of banbwidth.</A><BR><p>[This message has been edited by onfire (edited 11-30-2000).]||||reg||1
